PURPOSE

H144 is still primary-incomplete.

Its completed DAMAGE-25 block and the H143/H144 exploratory reconstruction work jointly expose a new architectural question.

De-novo topology reconstruction works only when:

RELATIONAL STRUCTURE
SURVIVES
LONG ENOUGH
TO SEED
RE-DEVELOPMENT.

A harsh exploratory H144-side probe then damaged:

- accepted topology;
- recent relational memory;

and allowed only:

0.5N fresh pair observations/episode,
8 recovery episodes,
10% sensor error.

At approximately the same protected-memory budget:

RANDOM RELATIONAL ANCHORS

produced:
mean ARI approximately 0.128.

COVERAGE-SCAFFOLD RELATIONAL ANCHORS

produced:
mean ARI approximately 0.993.

Thus:

MEMORY AMOUNT
WAS NOT
THE MAIN VARIABLE.

MEMORY TOPOLOGY
WAS.

H145 asks:

CAN
A VERY SMALL,
TOPOLOGY-INDEPENDENT
RELATIONAL SCAFFOLD

ACT AS:
A REGENERATIVE SEED

WHEN:

ACTIVE TOPOLOGY
AND
RECENT RELATIONAL MEMORY

ARE BOTH DAMAGED?

And equally important:

CAN THAT SCAFFOLD
AVOID
FOSSILIZING
AN OBSOLETE TOPOLOGY

AFTER:
A REAL SPLIT
OR:
A REAL MERGE?

BOUNDARY

Synthetic research only.

STRUCTURAL GERMLINE

is architectural shorthand for:

A SMALL,
PROTECTED,
TOPOLOGY-INDEPENDENT
SET OF RELATIONAL CONSTRAINTS

used only to:
seed or validate
structural regeneration.

It is not:
biological tissue,
DNA,
or:
an immutable execution authority.

The scaffold may itself:
age,
be challenged,
be invalidated,
and:
be replaced.

H145 does not:
- implement living tissue;
- claim biological equivalence;
- establish production memory budgets;
- execute or modify STAB-18-R1;
- spend canonical scientific execution;
- authorize runtime activation.

EVIDENCE CLASS TARGET

SYNTHETIC
MEASURED_SANDBOX
REGENERATIVE-MEMORY

PRIMARY FAMILY

Population:
70..100 members.

Latent groups:
4..6.

Primary recovery horizon:
12 episodes.

Fresh post-damage evidence:
0.5N pair observations/episode.

Primary sensor error:
10%.

Recent ordinary relational memory:
ERASED
at the damage event.

Accepted topology:
25% membership damage.

Thus the system retains only:

- member identity;
- member state;
- protected scaffold memory;
- new post-damage observations.

No original group label:
is available at reconstruction time.

PROTECTED MEMORY BUDGET

Primary:
2N signed pair relations.

Each scaffold edge stores:

{
    member_a,
    member_b,
    expected_relation = SAME | DIFFERENT,
    evidence_epoch,
    confidence_class
}

The edge does NOT store:
a subfamily identifier.

PRIMARY SCAFFOLDS

A — NONE

No protected relational scaffold.

Fresh post-damage evidence only.

B — RANDOM-2N

Protect:
2N uniformly sampled pair relations.

This is the H144 exploratory weak baseline.

C — POSITIVE-ONLY-2N

Coverage-constrained positive memory.

Each member is targeted by:
approximately two historically SAME relations.

No explicit negative relation coverage.

Tests whether:
within-group connectivity alone
is sufficient.

D — BALANCED-COVERAGE-2N

For each member,
preserve approximately:

- one SAME relation;
- one DIFFERENT relation.

Edges are deduplicated,
with spare budget used to repair:
under-covered members.

No reconstruction-time group label
is stored.

This is the primary candidate.

E — LANDMARK-SIGNATURE-2N

Choose a bounded set of historically representative members.

Store:
member-to-landmark
SAME/DIFFERENT signatures

within the same total edge budget.

Landmark identity is retained.

Landmark group label is not.

This tests:
compact relational coordinates.

F — RANDOM-REDUNDANT-2N

Random edges
but weighted toward:
members with low current scaffold degree.

This isolates:
coverage regularity

from:
signed SAME/DIFFERENT balancing.

RECONSTRUCTION

Use:
the H144 de-novo relational pipeline.

1. combine:
protected scaffold edges
+
fresh post-damage evidence;

2. build:
confidence-weighted positive affinity;

3. infer:
K = 3..8
from:
normalized-Laplacian eigengap;

4. spectral candidate reconstruction;

5. no original labels
and:
no latent K
are supplied.

PRIMARY DAMAGE TEST

True topology remains unchanged.

At damage time:

- accepted topology receives 25% membership corruption;
- recent ordinary relational memory is erased;
- protected scaffold survives subject to scaffold-damage condition.

Protected scaffold corruption:

0%,
10%,
25%.

Scaffold corruption means:
selected protected edges are:
erased
or:
their sign is flipped,
according to the preregistered corruption realization.

PRIMARY FOSSILIZATION TEST

A scaffold is created at epoch 0.

Then the TRUE topology changes.

Two regimes:

SPLIT-THEN-DAMAGE

1. create scaffold under old topology;
2. true near-equal split occurs;
3. collect 8 episodes of fresh current evidence;
4. erase recent ordinary memory;
5. damage accepted topology;
6. attempt regeneration.

MERGE-THEN-DAMAGE

Same,
but:
two groups truly merge.

The old protected scaffold is now:
partly stale.

PRIMARY STALENESS POLICIES

S0 — IMMUTABLE

Treat every scaffold edge as:
always valid.

Negative comparator.

S1 — EPOCH-GATED

A scaffold edge from an older topology epoch:
cannot directly authorize current reconstruction.

It may only act as:
a low-weight prior

until:
fresh evidence reconfirms it.

S2 — CONTRADICTION-CHALLENGED

Old scaffold edge starts:
provisionally usable.

When fresh current evidence directly contradicts it:

maintain:
contradiction debt.

After:
2 independent contradictory observations

the old edge is:
QUARANTINED
from reconstruction.

After:
3 consistent reconfirmations

an old edge may become:
CURRENT again.

S3 — DUAL-GENERATION

Maintain:
old scaffold
and:
one bounded candidate replacement scaffold.

New scaffold edges mature from:
fresh current relational evidence.

Old generation hibernates
until:
the candidate proves:
better reconstruction / validation.

No unbounded scaffold genealogy.

PRIMARY METRICS

1. reconstructed ARI;
2. correct inferred-K rate;
3. 10th-percentile ARI;
4. recovery episodes to ARI >=0.75;
5. protected edges/member;
6. scaffold storage cost;
7. scaffold sign-error rate after corruption;
8. stale-edge quarantine rate;
9. stale-edge false-retention rate;
10. obsolete-topology reconstruction rate;
11. fresh evidence consumed;
12. regeneration failure rate.

PRIMARY ACCEPTANCE SHAPE

The structural-germline thesis is supported if:

- BALANCED-COVERAGE-2N materially outperforms RANDOM-2N under primary DAMAGE-25;
- BALANCED-COVERAGE-2N reaches mean ARI >=0.85 under zero scaffold corruption;
- useful regeneration survives 10% scaffold corruption;
- correct-K rate materially exceeds RANDOM-2N;
- memory remains O(N), not O(N^2);
- IMMUTABLE scaffold shows measurable fossilization after true split or merge;
- EPOCH-GATED or CONTRADICTION-CHALLENGED materially reduces obsolete-topology reconstruction;
- staleness protection does not erase the regenerative advantage in unchanged-topology damage;
- DUAL-GENERATION remains bounded to one alternate scaffold generation;
- no reconstruction receives latent K or stored group labels.

FAILURE CONDITIONS

Treat H145 as mixed/negative if:

- balanced scaffold advantage disappears outside one seed/configuration;
- the scaffold only works because it covertly encodes group labels;
- 10% edge corruption destroys regeneration;
- protected memory must grow approximately quadratically;
- stale scaffold dominates fresh evidence after legitimate structural change;
- staleness rejection removes so much memory that NONE performs equivalently;
- dual-generation scaffold accumulates unbounded protected state.

